  "query_preview": "..\n\nAn upper-level trough is currently over the PNW. Embedded within \nthe trough is a shortwave digging over the Northern Rockies and \nGreat Plains region. A front associated with the shortwave is \npassing through NE Montana, causing a decrease in temperatures \nand gusty winds to veer W-NW early today. A Lake Wind Advisory is\ncurrently in effect until today at 9AM.\n\nThe shortwave and associated front exit to the east by late this \nmorning ahead of the main trough, which will begin digging over \nthe Northern Rockies this afternoon. From here, the trough will \nstall and remain quasi-stationary due to an omega blocking \npattern currently developing over the Midwest and Great Lakes \nregions.\n\nBy Sunday afternoon, the omega block will have decayed and the \nupper-level trough over Montana will begin exiting to the NE, \nleaving moisture-ladden SW flow aloft in its wake. Isolated \npockets of vorticity may generate rain showers in the southeast \nportions of the CWA Sunday afternoon and evening. Isolated\nThunderstorms may be possible SE of the Yellowstone River.\n\nSW flow aloft will gradually flatten and become semi-zonal over\nthe course of Monday, becoming truly zonal by Tuesday afternoon.\nFair conditions will persist through the week.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nConfidence in rain showers Sunday is low. Precipitation could \neasily occur in the western portions of North Dakota, rather than \nwithin our CWA. Wetting rain is unlikely.\n\n\nDON",
